- The coefficient of friction (\( \mu \)) is a dimensionless ratio. It is defined as the ratio of the force required to escape friction to the force acting on the surface.  
- Since it is a ratio of forces, it is dimensionless, meaning it has no physical units.  
- Therefore, the dimensional representation is \( [M^0L^0T^0] \), which means it has no units in terms of mass (\( M \)), length (\( L \)), or time (\( T \)).  

