\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the entropy change (\(\Delta c_i\)) for the given reaction, we use the Beer-Lambert law:

\[
\Delta c_i = \sum c_i \Delta c_i
\]

where \(c_i\) is the stoichiometric coefficient of each species and \(\Delta c_i\) is the molar entropy of each species.

For the given reaction:

\[
2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g)
\]

The molar solubilities are:

\[
\Delta c_1 = 91.2 \text{ (for } \text{PbS(s))} \\
\Delta c_2 = 205.1 \text{ (for } \text{O}_2(g)) \\
\Delta c_3 = 66.5 \text{ (for } \text{PbO(s))} \\
\Delta c_4 = 248.2 \text{ (for } \text{SO}_2(g))
\]

Substitute the known values into the equation:

\[
\Delta c_i = 2 \times 91.2 + 3 \times 205.1 - 2 \times 66.5 - 2 \times 248.2
\]

Calculate the values:

\[
\Delta c_i = 182.4 + 615.3 - 133 - 496.4
\]

Add the positive and subtract the negative values:

\[
\Delta c_i = 797.7 - 629.4 = 168.3
\]

Thus, the entropy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{168.3 \text{ g/mol K}}\).
