<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide (CaO) and water (H2O) to form calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)2), given that all species are in their standard state and that the balanced chemical equation is:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpies of formation of CaO(s), H2O(l) and Ca(OH)2(s) are -635.1 kJ/mol, -285.8 kJ/mol, and -986.2 kJ/mol respectively.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the Beer-Lambert's Law:

\[
\text{ΔH = Σ[H_f(products)] - Σ[H_f(products)]
\]

Where:
- ΔH is the standard enthalpy change of the reaction
- H_f is the standard enthalpy of formation of the substance
- The sum is taken over all the reactants and products in the reaction.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The balanced balanced chemical equation is:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpy of formation for each species is:
- H_f(CaO(s)) = -635.1 kcal/mol
- H_f(H2O(l)) = -285.8 kcal/mol
- H_f(Ca(OH)2(s)) = -986.2 kcal/mol

Now, we can calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction:

\[
\text{ΔH = H_f(Ca(OH)_2(s)) - [H_f(CaO(s)) + H_f(H_2O(l))]
\]

\[
\text{ΔH = -986.2 kcal/mol - [(-635.1 kcal/mol) + (-285.8 kcal/mol)]
\]

\[
\text{ΔH = -986.2 kcal/mol - (-920.9 kcal/mol)
\]

\[
\text{ΔH = -986.2 kcal/mol + 920.9 kcal/mol
\]

\[
\text{ΔH = -65.3 kcal/mol
\]

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-65.3 \text{ kcal/mol}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

