<USER_REQUEST>
What is the coordination geometry of the metal ion in the active site of hemoglobin, and how does this geometry enable the binding and release of oxygen molecules?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The coordination geometry of the metal ion (iron, Fe2+) in the active site of hemoglobin is octahedral. Hemoglobin is a protein responsible for transporting oxygen in the blood. It consists of four subunits, each containing a heme group with an iron (Fe2+) ion at its center.

In the deoxyhemoglobin state (without oxygen bound), the iron ion is coordinated to four nitrogen atoms from the porphyrin ring of the heme group, forming a square planar geometry. The fifth coordination site is occupied by a nitrogen atom from a histidine residue (called the proximal histidine) of the protein. This results in a distorted five-coordinate square pyramidal geometry.

