This implies $\sin \varphi=-\sin (\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+\varphi )$.  
Since $\sin \varphi=-\sin (\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+\varphi )$, we have $\sin \varphi=\cos (\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+\varphi )$.  
Since $\cos \varphi=\sin (\frac{\pi}{2}-\phi)$, we have $\sin \varphi=\sin (\frac{\pi}{2}-(\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+\varphi ))=\sin (\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{\omega \pi}{2}-\varphi )$.  
Therefore, $\varphi=\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{\omega \pi}{2}-\varphi$ or $\varphi=\2k\pi+\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{\omega \pi}{2}-\varphi$ (where $k\in\mathbb{Z}$).  
Solving these equations, we get $2\varphi=\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{\omega \pi}{2}$ or $2\varphi=\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{\omega \pi}{2}+2k\pi$.  
Thus, $\varphi=\frac{\pi}{4}-\frac{\omega \pi}{4}$ or $\varphi=\frac{\pi}{4}-\frac{\omega \pi}{4}+k\pi$.  
Since $0 < \varphi < \frac{\pi}{2}$, we have $\varphi=\frac{\pi}{4}-\frac{\omega \pi}{4}$.  
This implies $\omega=1$.  
Therefore, $f(x)=\sin (x+\varphi )$.  
The graph of $f(x)$ is translated to the right by $\frac{\pi}{12}$ units, resulting in the function $g(x)=f(x-\frac{\pi}{12})=\sin (x-\frac{\pi}{12}+\varphi )$.  
Since the graph of $g(x)$ is symmetric about the origin, $g(x)$ is an odd function, which implies $g(x)=-g(-x)$.  
Therefore, $\sin (x-\frac{\pi}{12}+\varphi )=-\sin (-x+\frac{\pi}{12}-\varphi )$.  
