\boxed{y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)}
\]  
This equation is derived from the definition of the derivative as the slope of the tangent line. The derivative \( f'(a) \) is the slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \), and \( (a, f(a)) \) is the point through which the tangent line passes. The slope-intercept form of a line is \( y = mx + b \), where \( m \) is the slope and \( b \) is the y-intercept. Applying the point-slope form of a line, which is \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \), where \( (x_1, y_1) \) is the point and \( m \) is the slope, we substitute \( m = f'(a) \), \( x_1 = a \), and \( y_1 = f(a) \) to get the equation:  
