\[
P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^k \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{n-k} = \binom{n}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n
\]

Here, \( n = 12 \) and \( p = \frac{1}{2} \), so:

\[
P(X = k) = \binom{12}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]

Now, we calculate the probabilities for \( k = 9, 10, 11, 12 \):

\[
P(X = 9) = \binom{12}{9} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 504 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]
\[
P(X = 10) = \binom{12}{10} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 66 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]
\[
P(X = 11) = \binom{12}{11} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 12 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]
\[
P(X = 12) = \binom{12}{12} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 1 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]

Summing these probabilities:

\[
P(X \geq 9) = P(X = 9) + P(X = 10) + P(X = 11) + P(X = 12) = \left(504 + 66 + 12 + 1\right) \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12} = 583 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12}
\]

