To find the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \), we first need to find the initial and final closed-loop gains.

The forward gain \( A_v = 100 \). The feedback ratio \( m_v = 0.1 \).

The closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 111.11 \text{ V^{-1}}
\]

The gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB, which means the new forward gain \( A_v' \) is:
\[
A_v' = A_v \times \frac{1}{2^6} = 100 \times \frac{1}{64} = 1.5625 \text{ V^{-1}}
\]

The new closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:
\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 - m_v} = \frac{1.5625}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{1.5625}{0.9} \approx 1.73 \text{ V^{-1}}
\]

The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage Change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{1.73 - 111.11}{111.11} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{-109.38}{111.11} \right) \times 100\% \approx -98.33\%
\]

Thus, the percent change in the closed-loop gain is approximately \(\boxed{-98.33\%}\).
